# Docker Compose variables for a CLIENT deployment — one tenant's
# user-facing half, pointed at a server deployment:
#
#   ./modules/backend/run_host_server.sh &   # leoserv; skip for text-only
#   docker compose --env-file config/client.env \
#       -f modules/backend/docker-compose.yml \
#       -f modules/context/docker-compose.yml \
#       -f modules/chat-ui/docker-compose.yml \
#       -f modules/monitoring/docker-compose.yml up -d --wait --pull always
#
# Only values Compose itself needs before any container exists belong in
# here: image tags, published ports, the project name. The tenant id and the
# server's address are NOT here — they are what the application reads, and
# they live in config/custom.config.
#
# Unlike the other two roles, the values below are set rather than
# commented out: a client deployment shares a machine with the server
# deployment it talks to (or with another tenant's client), so it cannot
# simply take the compose files' defaults.

# Compose project name, and the name of the Docker network this deployment's
# modules join. MUST differ from the server deployment's (cltl-platform) and
# from every other tenant's — it is what keeps the two from sharing a network
# and colliding. Keep it in step with the tenant in config/custom.config.
CLTL_PROJECT=cltl-platform-client-tenant-a

# Published host ports. Each must be free on this machine: 8001 is taken by a
# co-located server deployment's storage API, which is why the backend here
# is on 9001. A second tenant on this machine needs a different value for
# every one of these.
CLTL_BACKEND_PORT=9001
CLTL_CHATUI_PORT=8003
CLTL_MONITORING_PORT=8005

# Pin every image to one tag instead of :latest.
# VERSION=1.0.0
